@page "/"

<PageTitle>SOLID Showdown</PageTitle>

<section class="hero">
    <h1>SOLID, one principle at a time</h1>
    <p class="lead">
        Each principle has two or more worked examples. Every example puts a design that <span class="t-bad">breaks</span> the principle
        next to one that <span class="t-good">follows</span> it. You can apply real change requests to both and watch what each one
        has to touch, then run the actual C#.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="cards">
    @foreach (var p in SolidCatalog.All)
    {
        <a class="card" href="@($"principle/{p.Slug}")">
            <span class="card-letter">@p.Letter</span>
            <span class="card-body">
                <span class="card-name">@p.Name</span>
                <span class="card-line">@p.OneLiner</span>
                <span class="card-meta">@p.Examples.Count examples · @p.Quiz.Count quiz questions</span>
            </span>
        </a>
    }
</section>

<section class="how">
    <h2>How each page works</h2>
    <ol class="how-steps">
        <li><strong>Structure &amp; change impact.</strong> Class diagrams of both designs. Pick a change request (“add a format”, “the CFO changes overtime”) and every box is coloured by what happens to it: modified, added, re-tested or broken.</li>
        <li><strong>The code.</strong> The real source, side by side. ❌ and ✅ comments mark the key lines, and <em>🤔 CONTEXT</em> comments discuss when the rule does or doesn’t apply.</li>
        <li><strong>Run it.</strong> Both designs face the same checks. LSP runs contract tests against every subtype, and ISP calls every interface method through reflection to see which ones really work.</li>
        <li><strong>It depends.</strong> Notes on when the “bad” version is actually fine.</li>
    </ol>
</section>

<section class="depends">
    <h2>A note on “it depends”</h2>
    <p>
        SOLID principles are heuristics for managing <em>change</em>, not laws. Each one only pays off relative to some context:
        SRP depends on who the actors are, OCP is only ever closed against the kinds of change you anticipated, LSP depends on the
        contract you actually promise, ISP depends on what your clients use, and DIP is only worth it for volatile dependencies.
        Each page has a “Where it depends” panel, and several change scenarios deliberately show the <em>good</em> design
        having to change too.
    </p>
</section>
